1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,814
Sweetheart, the best cook on our street

2
00:00:02,814 --> 00:00:06,350
in nineteen fifty-five didn't speak much English.

3
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:07,459
Mrs.

4
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:09,876
Kowalski, from Poland,

5
00:00:09,876 --> 00:00:11,401
two doors down.

6
00:00:11,581 --> 00:00:14,204
Every Saturday, the smell of her cabbage

7
00:00:14,204 --> 00:00:16,695
rolls came through our kitchen window,

8
00:00:16,695 --> 00:00:18,990
and my brother Danny would stand on

9
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:21,023
the back porch and sniff like a

10
00:00:21,023 --> 00:00:23,318
hound dog until she waved him over.

11
00:00:23,668 --> 00:00:26,722
Today, people tell me their cabbage rolls

12
00:00:26,722 --> 00:00:28,361
fall apart in the pot,

13
00:00:28,361 --> 00:00:30,819
or the leaves are tough as paper,

14
00:00:30,819 --> 00:00:32,457
or the filling is dry.

15
00:00:32,637 --> 00:00:34,843
So tonight I'm giving you Mrs.

16
00:00:35,023 --> 00:00:36,892
Kowalski's cabbage rolls,

17
00:00:36,892 --> 00:00:39,734
every step, and the four mistakes that

18
00:00:39,734 --> 00:00:41,304
make them fall apart.

19
00:00:41,484 --> 00:00:42,663
And stay with me,

20
00:00:42,663 --> 00:00:45,091
because the trick for the leaves is

21
00:00:45,091 --> 00:00:47,796
the one that changed everything for me,

22
00:00:47,796 --> 00:00:49,530
and it uses your freezer.

23
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,413
In the steel towns of Ohio in

24
00:00:52,413 --> 00:00:55,906
the fifties, half the families came from

25
00:00:55,906 --> 00:00:59,226
Poland, Hungary, Slovakia and Ukraine.

26
00:00:59,406 --> 00:01:02,665
And every one of those grandmothers made

27
00:01:02,665 --> 00:01:03,806
cabbage rolls.

28
00:01:03,986 --> 00:01:06,416
The Poles called them golabki,

29
00:01:06,416 --> 00:01:07,631
little pigeons.

30
00:01:07,811 --> 00:01:11,341
The Hungarians called them toltott kaposzta.

31
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:12,473
Mrs.

32
00:01:12,653 --> 00:01:16,099
Kowalski said every grandmother thinks hers are

33
00:01:16,099 --> 00:01:17,566
the only right ones,

34
00:01:17,566 --> 00:01:19,839
and every grandmother is right.

35
00:01:20,190 --> 00:01:21,803
When I was ten,

36
00:01:21,803 --> 00:01:22,233
Mrs.

37
00:01:22,413 --> 00:01:24,282
Kowalski let me help.

38
00:01:24,462 --> 00:01:26,030
She sat me on a stool,

39
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:28,096
gave me a big bowl of filling

40
00:01:28,096 --> 00:01:30,589
and a stack of soft cabbage leaves,

41
00:01:30,589 --> 00:01:32,798
and showed me how to roll them,

42
00:01:32,798 --> 00:01:34,365
like a little package.

43
00:01:34,545 --> 00:01:36,758
My first ones looked like a dog

44
00:01:36,758 --> 00:01:37,901
had chewed them.

45
00:01:38,081 --> 00:01:40,533
She put them in the pot anyway,

46
00:01:40,533 --> 00:01:42,036
right next to hers,

47
00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:43,935
and when they were done,

48
00:01:43,935 --> 00:01:46,150
she said mine were the best.

49
00:01:46,330 --> 00:01:47,920
She lied beautifully.

50
00:01:48,271 --> 00:01:51,170
Cabbage rolls were what you made when

51
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:52,581
you had a cabbage,

52
00:01:52,581 --> 00:01:53,835
a pound of meat,

53
00:01:53,835 --> 00:01:55,480
and a family to feed.

54
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:58,020
One pound of ground meat and a

55
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:00,852
cup of rice makes sixteen big rolls,

56
00:02:00,852 --> 00:02:03,291
enough for a family of six with

57
00:02:03,291 --> 00:02:04,078
leftovers.

58
00:02:04,258 --> 00:02:06,353
In nineteen fifty-five,

59
00:02:06,353 --> 00:02:08,449
the whole pot cost Mrs.

60
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:10,649
Kowalski about a dollar.

61
00:02:10,829 --> 00:02:12,733
Today it's about twelve.

62
00:02:12,913 --> 00:02:15,688
Still the cheapest Sunday dinner I know.

63
00:02:16,038 --> 00:02:17,726
Here's what you need,

64
00:02:17,726 --> 00:02:18,209
honey.

65
00:02:18,389 --> 00:02:20,858
One large head of green cabbage,

66
00:02:20,858 --> 00:02:22,324
about three pounds.

67
00:02:22,504 --> 00:02:24,719
One pound of ground beef,

68
00:02:24,719 --> 00:02:27,110
or half beef and half pork,

69
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:28,704
which is what Mrs.

70
00:02:28,884 --> 00:02:30,695
Kowalski used.

71
00:02:30,875 --> 00:02:32,779
Pork makes them juicier.

72
00:02:32,959 --> 00:02:35,052
One cup of white rice,

73
00:02:35,052 --> 00:02:35,908
uncooked.

74
00:02:36,088 --> 00:02:38,491
One onion, chopped fine.

75
00:02:38,671 --> 00:02:40,529
Two cloves of garlic.

76
00:02:40,709 --> 00:02:41,731
One egg.

77
00:02:41,911 --> 00:02:45,568
Salt, pepper, and a teaspoon of paprika.

78
00:02:45,918 --> 00:02:47,137
For the sauce.

79
00:02:47,317 --> 00:02:49,953
One big can of crushed tomatoes,

80
00:02:49,953 --> 00:02:51,601
twenty-eight ounces.

81
00:02:51,781 --> 00:02:53,728
One can of tomato soup,

82
00:02:53,728 --> 00:02:56,946
the way every Ohio grandmother did it,

83
00:02:56,946 --> 00:02:59,571
or a small can of tomato sauce.

84
00:02:59,751 --> 00:03:02,177
A tablespoon of brown sugar.

85
00:03:02,357 --> 00:03:05,736
A tablespoon of vinegar or lemon juice.

86
00:03:05,916 --> 00:03:08,099
And a cup of water or broth.

87
00:03:08,279 --> 00:03:10,794
Some families add a jar of sauerkraut

88
00:03:10,794 --> 00:03:12,493
on the bottom of the pot.

89
00:03:12,673 --> 00:03:13,706
Mrs.

90
00:03:13,886 --> 00:03:16,731
Kowalski did, and so do I.

91
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:18,176
Now the leaves,

92
00:03:18,176 --> 00:03:20,658
and this is where most people give

93
00:03:20,658 --> 00:03:20,877
up.

94
00:03:21,057 --> 00:03:22,898
The old way is to boil the

95
00:03:22,898 --> 00:03:25,022
whole head of cabbage in a big

96
00:03:25,022 --> 00:03:27,287
pot, and peel off the leaves one

97
00:03:27,287 --> 00:03:28,845
by one as they soften,

98
00:03:28,845 --> 00:03:31,181
with tongs, burning your fingers.

99
00:03:31,361 --> 00:03:32,499
Mrs.

100
00:03:32,679 --> 00:03:35,169
Kowalski did it that way her whole

101
00:03:35,169 --> 00:03:35,535
life.

102
00:03:35,715 --> 00:03:38,025
It works, but it's a fight.

103
00:03:38,205 --> 00:03:40,167
Here's the trick I promised you.

104
00:03:40,518 --> 00:03:42,503
Put the whole head of cabbage in

105
00:03:42,503 --> 00:03:44,302
the freezer the night before.

106
00:03:44,482 --> 00:03:46,645
Then take it out in the morning

107
00:03:46,645 --> 00:03:48,738
and let it thaw on the counter

108
00:03:48,738 --> 00:03:49,576
for the day,

109
00:03:49,576 --> 00:03:51,669
or in the icebox for two days.

110
00:03:51,849 --> 00:03:52,878
When it thaws,

111
00:03:52,878 --> 00:03:55,450
the leaves come off soft and bendy,

112
00:03:55,450 --> 00:03:57,067
like they were cooked,

113
00:03:57,067 --> 00:03:58,536
and they don't tear.

114
00:03:58,716 --> 00:04:01,561
No boiling, no burned fingers.

115
00:04:01,741 --> 00:04:04,595
My daughter-in-law taught me that in nineteen

116
00:04:04,595 --> 00:04:06,689
ninety, and I wished I'd known it

117
00:04:06,689 --> 00:04:07,894
forty years sooner.

118
00:04:08,243 --> 00:04:10,991
Either way, take your soft leaves and

119
00:04:10,991 --> 00:04:13,218
look at the thick rib down the

120
00:04:13,218 --> 00:04:14,629
middle of each one.

121
00:04:14,809 --> 00:04:16,353
With a small knife,

122
00:04:16,353 --> 00:04:18,385
shave that rib down flat,

123
00:04:18,385 --> 00:04:20,579
so it's as thin as the rest

124
00:04:20,579 --> 00:04:21,554
of the leaf.

125
00:04:21,734 --> 00:04:23,046
Don't cut it out.

126
00:04:23,226 --> 00:04:24,805
Just shave it thin.

127
00:04:24,985 --> 00:04:27,960
That thick rib is mistake number one,

128
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:30,936
the reason rolls crack and won't stay

129
00:04:30,936 --> 00:04:31,498
closed.

130
00:04:31,848 --> 00:04:34,245
And pick the right cabbage at the

131
00:04:34,245 --> 00:04:34,681
store.

132
00:04:34,861 --> 00:04:36,203
Look for a big,

133
00:04:36,203 --> 00:04:39,334
heavy head with loose outer leaves,

134
00:04:39,334 --> 00:04:40,408
not a small,

135
00:04:40,408 --> 00:04:41,839
tight, hard one.

136
00:04:42,019 --> 00:04:44,089
The loose ones have bigger,

137
00:04:44,089 --> 00:04:46,466
softer leaves that roll easier.

138
00:04:46,646 --> 00:04:49,403
Savoy cabbage, the crinkly kind,

139
00:04:49,403 --> 00:04:51,386
is even easier to roll,

140
00:04:51,386 --> 00:04:53,368
but it's more delicate.

141
00:04:53,548 --> 00:04:54,523
Mrs.

142
00:04:54,703 --> 00:04:57,719
Kowalski squeezed every cabbage at the market

143
00:04:57,719 --> 00:04:59,730
like she was checking a melon.

144
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:01,532
Now the filling.

145
00:05:01,712 --> 00:05:04,061
Cook the chopped onion in a little

146
00:05:04,061 --> 00:05:06,341
butter until it's soft and sweet,

147
00:05:06,341 --> 00:05:07,723
about eight minutes,

148
00:05:07,723 --> 00:05:08,829
and let it cool.

149
00:05:09,009 --> 00:05:10,613
Then in a big bowl,

150
00:05:10,613 --> 00:05:11,711
mix the meat,

151
00:05:11,711 --> 00:05:13,232
the uncooked rice,

152
00:05:13,232 --> 00:05:14,668
the cooled onion,

153
00:05:14,668 --> 00:05:16,357
the garlic, the egg,

154
00:05:16,357 --> 00:05:18,891
a teaspoon and a half of salt,

155
00:05:18,891 --> 00:05:22,016
pepper, paprika, and a quarter cup of

156
00:05:22,016 --> 00:05:23,451
the tomato sauce.

157
00:05:23,631 --> 00:05:25,277
Mix it with your hands,

158
00:05:25,277 --> 00:05:27,637
gently, just until it's together.

159
00:05:27,986 --> 00:05:29,682
If this is helping you,

160
00:05:29,682 --> 00:05:32,189
honey, tap the like button for me.

161
00:05:32,369 --> 00:05:35,225
It helps Grandma more than you know.

162
00:05:35,405 --> 00:05:38,018
And now, mistake number two.

163
00:05:38,198 --> 00:05:39,393
Cooked rice.

164
00:05:39,573 --> 00:05:41,655
People cook the rice first,

165
00:05:41,655 --> 00:05:43,429
and then it gets mushy,

166
00:05:43,429 --> 00:05:45,820
and the filling turns to paste.

167
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:47,811
Use raw rice.

168
00:05:47,991 --> 00:05:50,278
It cooks inside the roll and soaks

169
00:05:50,278 --> 00:05:52,297
up all the juice from the meat

170
00:05:52,297 --> 00:05:53,238
and the sauce.

171
00:05:53,418 --> 00:05:55,903
That's what keeps the filling moist.

172
00:05:56,252 --> 00:05:58,238
Step three, the rolling.

173
00:05:58,418 --> 00:06:00,517
Lay a leaf flat with the stem

174
00:06:00,517 --> 00:06:01,602
end toward you,

175
00:06:01,602 --> 00:06:03,991
the inside of the leaf facing up.

176
00:06:04,171 --> 00:06:06,030
Put about a quarter cup of filling

177
00:06:06,030 --> 00:06:07,015
near the stem end.

178
00:06:07,195 --> 00:06:09,950
Fold the bottom up over the filling,

179
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:11,786
then fold in both sides,

180
00:06:11,786 --> 00:06:13,775
and roll it away from you,

181
00:06:13,775 --> 00:06:15,229
snug but not tight.

182
00:06:15,409 --> 00:06:17,106
Like a little package,

183
00:06:17,106 --> 00:06:18,108
or a burrito,

184
00:06:18,108 --> 00:06:19,728
my grandchildren say.

185
00:06:20,077 --> 00:06:21,993
Mistake number three.

186
00:06:22,173 --> 00:06:23,938
Rolling them too tight.

187
00:06:24,118 --> 00:06:26,245
The rice swells as it cooks,

188
00:06:26,245 --> 00:06:28,447
and if the roll is too tight,

189
00:06:28,447 --> 00:06:30,422
it bursts open in the pot.

190
00:06:30,602 --> 00:06:32,239
Leave a little room.

191
00:06:32,419 --> 00:06:34,207
And don't overfill them.

192
00:06:34,387 --> 00:06:35,761
A quarter cup,

193
00:06:35,761 --> 00:06:36,546
no more.

194
00:06:36,726 --> 00:06:37,783
Mrs.

195
00:06:37,963 --> 00:06:40,830
Kowalski said, a cabbage roll is like

196
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:41,759
a good coat.

197
00:06:41,939 --> 00:06:43,086
It should fit,

198
00:06:43,086 --> 00:06:45,626
but you should still be able to

199
00:06:45,626 --> 00:06:46,282
breathe.

200
00:06:46,631 --> 00:06:47,978
Step four.

201
00:06:48,158 --> 00:06:50,496
Take the torn leaves and the small

202
00:06:50,496 --> 00:06:51,871
ones you didn't use,

203
00:06:51,871 --> 00:06:53,796
and line the bottom of a big

204
00:06:53,796 --> 00:06:55,171
heavy pot with them,

205
00:06:55,171 --> 00:06:57,853
with the sauerkraut if you're using it.

206
00:06:58,033 --> 00:07:01,016
That bed keeps the rolls from scorching.

207
00:07:01,196 --> 00:07:03,653
Then lay the rolls on top,

208
00:07:03,653 --> 00:07:05,071
seam side down,

209
00:07:05,071 --> 00:07:07,150
packed close together,

210
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:08,378
side by side,

211
00:07:08,378 --> 00:07:09,323
in layers.

212
00:07:09,503 --> 00:07:12,534
Packed close, they hold each other closed.

213
00:07:12,883 --> 00:07:15,397
So far, the freezer trick,

214
00:07:15,397 --> 00:07:16,944
shaving the rib,

215
00:07:16,944 --> 00:07:19,167
raw rice, a loose roll,

216
00:07:19,167 --> 00:07:21,391
and packing them close,

217
00:07:21,391 --> 00:07:22,357
seam down.

218
00:07:22,537 --> 00:07:24,781
Now mix the crushed tomatoes,

219
00:07:24,781 --> 00:07:26,715
tomato soup, brown sugar,

220
00:07:26,715 --> 00:07:28,108
vinegar and water,

221
00:07:28,108 --> 00:07:30,197
and pour it over the rolls,

222
00:07:30,197 --> 00:07:32,673
until it just about covers them.

223
00:07:32,853 --> 00:07:34,925
Cover the top with a few more

224
00:07:34,925 --> 00:07:36,998
cabbage leaves, then the lid.

225
00:07:37,347 --> 00:07:38,416
Step five.

226
00:07:38,596 --> 00:07:40,942
Bring it to a gentle simmer on

227
00:07:40,942 --> 00:07:43,523
the stove, then cook it very low,

228
00:07:43,523 --> 00:07:46,026
covered, for an hour and a half.

229
00:07:46,206 --> 00:07:48,091
Or put it in a three hundred

230
00:07:48,091 --> 00:07:50,246
fifty degree oven for two hours.

231
00:07:50,426 --> 00:07:52,713
And here's mistake number four.

232
00:07:52,893 --> 00:07:54,403
A hard boil.

233
00:07:54,583 --> 00:07:57,902
Boiling tosses the rolls around and breaks

234
00:07:57,902 --> 00:07:58,693
them open.

235
00:07:58,873 --> 00:07:59,558
Gentle.

236
00:07:59,738 --> 00:08:01,026
Barely bubbling.

237
00:08:01,206 --> 00:08:02,425
Don't stir it.

238
00:08:02,605 --> 00:08:04,846
Just shake the pot a little now

239
00:08:04,846 --> 00:08:05,496
and then.

240
00:08:05,846 --> 00:08:07,391
How do you know they're done?

241
00:08:07,571 --> 00:08:09,651
In a minute you'll smell it,

242
00:08:09,651 --> 00:08:12,029
honey, but here's the real test.

243
00:08:12,209 --> 00:08:13,637
Take one roll out,

244
00:08:13,637 --> 00:08:14,827
cut it in half,

245
00:08:14,827 --> 00:08:17,445
and taste the rice in the middle.

246
00:08:17,625 --> 00:08:19,999
If it's soft all the way through,

247
00:08:19,999 --> 00:08:20,934
they're done.

248
00:08:21,114 --> 00:08:23,086
If it's still a little hard,

249
00:08:23,086 --> 00:08:25,409
give them another twenty minutes.

250
00:08:25,589 --> 00:08:27,857
The cabbage should be so tender you

251
00:08:27,857 --> 00:08:29,606
can cut it with the side of

252
00:08:29,606 --> 00:08:30,059
a fork.

253
00:08:30,409 --> 00:08:32,799
Lift them out gently with a big

254
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:35,112
spoon, two or three per plate,

255
00:08:35,112 --> 00:08:37,039
and spoon the sauce over.

256
00:08:37,219 --> 00:08:38,252
Mrs.

257
00:08:38,432 --> 00:08:41,000
Kowalski put a spoon of sour cream

258
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,568
on top and served them with mashed

259
00:08:43,568 --> 00:08:45,305
potatoes and rye bread.

260
00:08:45,485 --> 00:08:48,248
My father ate four and called them

261
00:08:48,248 --> 00:08:50,280
Polish pigs in a blanket,

262
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:50,930
and Mrs.

263
00:08:51,110 --> 00:08:53,802
Kowalski would shake her wooden spoon at

264
00:08:53,802 --> 00:08:54,744
him and laugh.

265
00:08:55,094 --> 00:08:58,201
Cabbage rolls are even better the next

266
00:08:58,201 --> 00:08:59,100
day, honey.

267
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:01,358
Every grandmother knows it.

268
00:09:01,538 --> 00:09:03,928
Keep them in the sauce in the

269
00:09:03,928 --> 00:09:05,659
icebox for four days,

270
00:09:05,659 --> 00:09:07,390
and warm them slowly.

271
00:09:07,570 --> 00:09:09,641
They also freeze beautifully,

272
00:09:09,641 --> 00:09:10,570
in the sauce,

273
00:09:10,570 --> 00:09:11,784
for three months.

274
00:09:11,964 --> 00:09:14,446
I make a double pot in January

275
00:09:14,446 --> 00:09:15,770
and freeze half,

276
00:09:15,770 --> 00:09:18,583
and on a cold Tuesday in February,

277
00:09:18,583 --> 00:09:20,486
dinner is already done.

278
00:09:20,835 --> 00:09:22,953
And if you don't have time for

279
00:09:22,953 --> 00:09:25,212
rolling, make unstuffed cabbage.

280
00:09:25,392 --> 00:09:27,595
Brown the meat and onion in a

281
00:09:27,595 --> 00:09:29,494
pot, add chopped cabbage,

282
00:09:29,494 --> 00:09:30,861
the uncooked rice,

283
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:33,291
the sauce and two cups of water,

284
00:09:33,291 --> 00:09:36,329
and simmer it covered for forty minutes.

285
00:09:36,509 --> 00:09:38,636
Same taste, one pot,

286
00:09:38,636 --> 00:09:39,806
no rolling.

287
00:09:39,986 --> 00:09:43,278
My granddaughter calls it lazy cabbage rolls,

288
00:09:43,278 --> 00:09:45,327
and she makes it every week.

289
00:09:45,676 --> 00:09:47,135
So one more time,

290
00:09:47,135 --> 00:09:47,650
honey.

291
00:09:47,830 --> 00:09:50,449
Freeze the cabbage the night before,

292
00:09:50,449 --> 00:09:52,486
so the leaves come off soft.

293
00:09:52,666 --> 00:09:53,815
Shave the rib.

294
00:09:53,995 --> 00:09:55,946
Raw rice in the filling.

295
00:09:56,126 --> 00:09:57,361
A quarter cup,

296
00:09:57,361 --> 00:09:59,655
rolled snug but not tight.

297
00:09:59,835 --> 00:10:02,050
A bed of leaves in the pot,

298
00:10:02,050 --> 00:10:04,758
rolls packed close and seam down.

299
00:10:04,938 --> 00:10:07,569
Tomato sauce with a little sugar and

300
00:10:07,569 --> 00:10:08,154
vinegar.

301
00:10:08,334 --> 00:10:09,702
Low and slow,

302
00:10:09,702 --> 00:10:11,596
never a hard boil.

303
00:10:11,776 --> 00:10:13,134
That's Mrs.

304
00:10:13,314 --> 00:10:15,540
Kowalski's cabbage rolls,

305
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:17,854
and they never fall apart.

306
00:10:18,203 --> 00:10:20,058
Tell me in the comments,

307
00:10:20,058 --> 00:10:20,909
sweetheart.

308
00:10:21,089 --> 00:10:23,457
What did your grandmother call them?

309
00:10:23,637 --> 00:10:26,263
Golabki, pigs in a blanket,

310
00:10:26,263 --> 00:10:28,014
or something else?

311
00:10:28,194 --> 00:10:29,912
I read every comment.

312
00:10:30,092 --> 00:10:33,037
The whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

313
00:10:33,037 --> 00:10:34,930
right there on your screen,

314
00:10:34,930 --> 00:10:36,893
and I'll see you in the next

315
00:10:36,893 --> 00:10:37,314
video.

316
00:10:37,494 --> 00:10:39,084
Good night, honey.
